</style><body><div class="notice">Review draft v0.2 · Oracle corrections applied · Founder review pending · No participant results</div><header><strong>MOSAIC</strong><nav aria-label="Page navigation"><a href="#what">What it is</a><a href="#how">How it works</a><a href="#start">Start a decision</a><a href="#limits">Limits</a></nav></header><main><p class="eyebrow">A FRAMEWORK FOR DECISIONS</p><h1>Make a choice you can explain.</h1><a href="#start">Start with your decision →</a><h2 id="what">Home</h2><p><strong>MOSAIC: make a choice you can explain.</strong></p><p>MOSAIC is a framework for deciding what to do when evidence is incomplete, scattered or conflicting. It helps you connect a choice to the evidence, assumptions and reasons behind it. You remain responsible for the decision.</p><p><strong>Who is it for?</strong> People responsible for a decision, and teams helping them examine it. It may be useful when explanations compete, sources disagree or consequences matter. This is intended-use wording, not evidence of effectiveness in a profession.</p><p><strong>What do you produce?</strong> An inspectable decision record: the choice, its reasons, alternatives considered, remaining uncertainty and what would make you reconsider.</p><p><a href="#start">Start with your decision →</a> · <a href="#how">See the six forms of reasoning</a></p><h2 id="how">How it works</h2><p>Use six forms of reasoning, revisiting them as gaps emerge. They are not a fixed one-way sequence.</p><p><strong>Meaning:</strong> What decision are we making? Name the owner, options, desired outcome, deadline and constraints. Produce a clear decision statement.</p><p><strong>Observations:</strong> What material do we have? Separate observations, reports, inferences and assumptions. Record origins and gaps.</p><p><strong>Structure:</strong> How does it connect? Link sources to claims and options. Preserve contradictions and shared origins.</p><p><strong>Analysis:</strong> What does the evidence support? Examine competing explanations and assumptions. Record findings and limits.</p><p><strong>Interpretation:</strong> What does that mean for our options? Consider consequences, risks and trade-offs, including uncertainty that could change the choice.</p><p><strong>Choice:</strong> What will we do, and why? Commit to the course best supported in context, and record the reasons, rejected alternatives, uncertainty and review conditions. Targeted inquiry may be warranted when its value justifies its cost.</p><h2 id="example">Concrete glimpse — provisional</h2><p>Loose reference to the current site&#x27;s product-strategy illustration, not an endorsed worked example or an observed result: a team sees reports of navigation problems. Assume only that the team has reports of navigation problems; their independence, the cause and the reversibility of any change have not been established. Before deciding what to change, it asks whether reports are independent or repeat one source, and whether the evidence supports a small reversible change or a larger commitment. MOSAIC keeps those questions connected to the choice and its reasons. This sketch does not establish the correct action. Full six-form example refinement remains future work.</p><h2 id="start">Start a decision</h2><p>Write: “By [date], [owner] needs to choose between [options], to achieve [outcome], within [constraints].” Then list what you know, its sources and which uncertainty could change the choice. Use the six forms to examine it; retain reasons and review conditions with the choice. This prompt is an editorial aid, not another MOSAIC stage or a prescribed record format.</p><h2 id="limits">About / FAQ</h2><p><strong>Does MOSAIC decide for me?</strong> No. Human judgement and responsibility remain.</p><p><strong>Does it guarantee a good outcome?</strong> No. Preserve what was known at decision time and learn from later outcomes; a reasoned choice can still have an adverse outcome.</p><p><strong>Is it a score?</strong> No canonical numerical scoring method is established here. Existing demonstration counts and weights are teaching devices.</p><p><strong>Why is Analysis also outside MOSAIC?</strong> External analytical work supplies material. Internal Analysis examines structured evidence for this decision. Operational systems implement the chosen action.</p><p><strong>How established is it?</strong> The public framework is developing. This draft explains a founder-confirmed website baseline, not measured effectiveness.</p></main><footer>Editorial prototype based on the founder-confirmed website baseline, 27 September 2026. The provisional illustration is not an endorsed teaching application. No information is collected or sent by this page.<details><summary>Reviewer provenance (exclude from participant copy)</summary><p>Core reference v0.2: SHE-2, document core-reference, revision 3995e839-f6b7-40ef-93fd-ec2de31c9b32. Founder S10 decision: SHE-2, document open-questions, revision 7856dfa9-539b-466d-9504-68e461a80764, interaction 77867ccc-219e-45fa-bb19-68e122fecdd0. These identifiers are provided for recovery in Paperclip when viewing this downloaded file.</p><p>Glimpse: loose S3 scene; shared-origin caution C8 (S4, with S7 only as illustration); reversible-versus-larger choice is inference I2 grounded in C1/C10; all example use bounded by S10.</p><p>Oracle checklist: SHE-3, document oracle-accuracy-review, revision d7fba4d2-71f9-4848-9baa-101926fa7418. It reviewed v0.1; this v0.2 applies its specified corrections. External navigation is disallowed during assessment.</p></details></footer></body></html>